#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406f464-6326-5dbf-b35c-8cde390a65de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine or Cosine of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8b9ae0a-cc32-558d-9b59-f7c7608a4eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3863" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sin{270^\\circ}$." :question/worked-solution "The angle $270^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $270^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{270^\\circ} = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b547a211-99ec-5325-8d76-da3f7da13174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent or Cotangent of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "33f8f1c3-eeca-5092-804d-ab6cd468d5c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3864" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "23de6cbe-0e4f-508e-a955-0d48dbdd93ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3865" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sec \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,1)$.\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)}} = \\dfrac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569477} {:answer/id #uuid "65a2005c-6f9c-5b3b-92b5-b91a280aa805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569600} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac225ab-a108-5cf1-b663-e25ab4138d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569646} {:answer/id #uuid "80712ae4-c0c2-56af-a31a-939b00f59f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569657} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fae09-f797-5025-b37c-64365f7ed7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e33752-dbec-5be0-abda-0b23cc352083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569477} :answer-field/id #uuid "99de1166-6614-59dc-ba88-9b3adbcbf5e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "621a7d94-aa15-5da2-a86e-6e37bead83f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123585" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${90}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${90}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({90}^{∘})=-1+1=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569459} {:answer/id #uuid "42d752c4-1ced-5442-9d14-2e028d4482e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569535} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0f6439-02a9-5ffc-b967-796d14fd99c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569575} {:answer/id #uuid "a04922be-e134-5966-b10b-e756217fb61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569698} {:answer/id #uuid "b00a2584-97c8-50c5-81c4-5f4529530f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08429320-9580-541b-a6d9-31a45aa346b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569459} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba2b05c-984d-5188-ba82-e7eff754bb83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dc0c374-75b0-5038-a236-6349d3712634" :question/math-academy-id "q-23418" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406f464-6326-5dbf-b35c-8cde390a65de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine or Cosine of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "dc9edcfc-589f-5ca4-8a40-2ce180d59be8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a236920-488d-5d06-a1f4-5237d538b8be" :question/math-academy-id "q-262318" :question/problem "$\\sin (90^{∘}) =$ {{field